rket pricing reinforce that position; Iowa's Republican lean and the open-seat dynamic keep the expected margin near six rather than at the top of the polling range.", "watch": "A late shift in the polling average toward Lahn, or Republican outside spending closing the money gap, would move this race back toward tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Kim Reynolds (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Iowa's governorship is open for the first time in years with Kim Reynolds retiring, and Rob Sand enters the final stretch as a clear favorite over Zach Lahn. Polls from more than a dozen surveyors since late September put Sand ahead by four to twelve points, averaging near eight. Independent Sondra Wilson is on the ballot but registers little support.\n\nSand has shattered state fundraising records, raising $81 million with a large cash advantage, and prediction markets price his chances in the mid-to-high 80s. Most race raters call the contest Lean Democratic, a notch below the polling, because Iowa leans Republican by six points and open seats can shift quickly. The race sits at a six-point Democratic edge, with enough residual uncertainty that a late Republican surge cannot be ruled out.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.8753, "margin": 5.7}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8859, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8669, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8657, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8472, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8297, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8734, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.8592, "margin": 5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.8769, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.8877, "margin": 6.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.8875, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.895, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.8938, "margin": 6.5}, {"date": "2026-10-05", "p_dem":